from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('24');add=A.add
add('5.4-01','Electrical power is the rate of electrical energy transfer.','It is measured in watts, with 1 W = 1 J/s.','An appliance’s power describes transfer rate rather than energy used over its whole operating period. A 20 W lamp transfers 20 J each second at its rated operating condition. Total energy also depends on duration. Energy transferred to light, motion or heating must still be accounted for as energy rather than new energy creation.','Define electrical power.','It is the rate at which electrical energy is transferred.')
add('5.4-02','For steady DC quantities, P = VI and E = Pt = VIt.','The relations connect energy per charge with charge flow per time.','Multiplying V in J/C by I in C/s gives J/s, or watts. Multiplying by seconds gives energy in joules. Use the stated school DC model; an AC appliance rating cannot generally be analysed by multiplying arbitrary instantaneous or peak values without considering the appropriate quantities. Here rated power supplies the mains energy calculation directly.','Explain how P = VI and E = Pt combine into E = VIt.','Link voltage times current to energy per second, giving power.~Multiplying that rate by the operating time gives E = VIt.')
add('5.4-02-M01','Steady electrical power is P = VI.','Use volts and amperes for watts.','A low-voltage motor at 6 V carrying 0.50 A has electrical input power 3 W. Its useful mechanical output can be smaller because other energy transfers occur. Identify input or output when discussing efficiency.','Calculate power for a 12 V component carrying 0.40 A.','P = VI = 12 × 0.40.~The power is 4.8 W.')
add('5.4-02-M02','For constant V and I over time t, E = VIt.','Use seconds for joules when voltage and current are in SI units.','A 6 V heater carrying 2 A for 30 s receives 360 J. If the readings vary appreciably, one simple product of the starting values does not represent the whole interval accurately. A joulemeter or correctly averaged measurement is then preferable.','Calculate energy transferred at 6 V and 0.50 A over 20 s.','E = VIt = 6 × 0.50 × 20.~The energy is 60 J.')
add('5.4-02-M03','At constant power, energy is E = Pt.','Watts times seconds gives joules; kilowatts times hours gives kilowatt-hours.','A 0.80 kW appliance running for 2 h uses 1.6 kWh. A kilowatt-hour is energy, not power or power per hour. One kWh equals 3.6 × 10⁶ J because it represents 1000 J/s for 3600 s. Keep the chosen unit path consistent.','Calculate the energy used by a 0.50 kW appliance running for 3 h.','E = Pt = 0.50 × 3.~The energy is 1.5 kWh.')
add('5.4-03','A rated power describes an appliance’s energy-transfer rate at its stated operating conditions.','A kilowatt-hour is the energy transferred by 1 kW operating for 1 h.','A 60 W lamp does not use 60 J in total regardless of duration. It transfers about 60 J each second at its rating. Actual energy use can vary with duty cycle, control setting and operating condition, so a refrigerator’s compressor rating is not proof that it runs continuously for a whole day.','State the meaning of a 40 W lamp rating at its rated supply.','It transfers electrical energy at 40 J/s under the stated rated conditions.')
add('5.4-04','Energy cost is energy used in kWh multiplied by the stated price per kWh.','A tariff used in a practice problem is an invented given, not a current electricity price.','At an invented N$2.40 per kWh, 1.5 kWh costs N$3.60 before any other charges. Convert watts to kilowatts and minutes to hours before forming kWh. A bill can include fixed charges, but include those only when the problem specifies them.','Calculate the energy-only cost of a 1.2 kW appliance used for 2 h at an invented N$2.50 per kWh.','Energy = 1.2 × 2 = 2.4 kWh.~Cost = 2.4 × 2.50 = N$6.00.')
add('5.5-01','The syllabus compares mains as a 220 V AC model with low-voltage DC from classroom batteries.','The 220 V value here is the syllabus’s given model, not a new specification of local supply conditions.','AC reverses direction while battery DC has one direction. Mains can deliver dangerous current and is not used for the classroom experiments in these notes. The low-voltage investigations use suitable current-limited supplies; a battery can still overheat or damage equipment if short-circuited.','Explain the syllabus-model difference between mains and a classroom battery supply.','The mains model is AC at the stated 220 V.~A classroom cell supply is typically lower-voltage DC with one-direction output.',heading='Mains and battery supply models')
add('5.5-02','Electrical devices transfer energy into heating, lighting or motion and can use magnetic switching.','The desired service determines which output is useful.','A heater primarily raises internal energy, a lamp supplies useful light, and a motor supplies mechanical work. An electric bell uses repeated magnetic attraction and release, while a relay changes a contact state. Device diagrams should distinguish the energy path from the control path.','Explain the connection between electrical input and useful outputs of a heater and motor.','Link heater input to increased internal energy of the intended region.~A motor converts part of its electrical input into mechanical output, with other transfers as losses.')
add('5.5-02-M01','A resistive heater transfers electrical energy to internal energy.','Its useful role is heating the intended material or surroundings.','A heating element is chosen to tolerate its operating temperature and power. Heat must be transferred to the intended load without overheating the element or nearby material. A classroom demonstration uses a rated low-voltage heater rather than exposed mains conductors.','Describe the energy conversion in a resistive electric heater.','Electrical energy transfers into internal energy of the element and then the intended heated material.')
add('5.5-02-M02','Parallel lighting puts each lamp branch across the same supply potential difference.','Each branch can have its own switch and one open branch need not interrupt the others.','In a series chain, a break stops the shared path and lamps share voltage according to their properties. Parallel connection avoids that dependence for ordinary separately controlled lighting. Trace the nodes to recognise branches rather than relying on the physical spacing of lamps.','Describe why parallel connection is useful for independently controlled lamps.','Each branch receives the supply voltage and can be switched without opening the other lamp paths.')
add('5.5-02-M03','An electric motor converts electrical input into mechanical output.','Magnetic forces on current-carrying conductors provide its turning effect.','Useful applications include fans and pumps. Heating in wires, friction and sound can reduce the useful fraction of input energy. A fan’s moving air is a useful mechanical output, not a direct creation of coldness.','Describe an electrical motor’s energy conversion.','It converts part of its electrical input into mechanical motion.')
add('5.5-02-M04','A basic electric bell uses an electromagnet and a make-break contact to sustain repeated impacts.','A relay uses an electromagnet to operate separate switch contacts.','In a bell, attraction moves an armature and hammer, breaks the coil circuit, and lets a spring return the armature and reconnect it. A relay need not oscillate: its contacts can remain operated while coil current is present. Distinguish a repeated bell action from the general switching purpose.','Describe how a simple electric bell repeats its action.','Coil attraction breaks its own contact, the spring restores it and the circuit reconnects, repeating the hammer movement.')
add('5.5-03','Damaged insulation, excessive current and damp conditions create different electrical hazards.','Protection must address contact, overheating and fault paths rather than relying on one device for every risk.','Exposed live metal can provide a path through a person. An overloaded cable can heat beyond its intended limit, damaging insulation or causing fire. Moisture can lower the resistance of unintended paths. Use these mechanisms to explain hazards in diagrams; learners do not need to create an unsafe circuit to demonstrate them.','Explain how an overloaded socket and damaged insulation can produce different dangers.','Link overload to excessive current and heating of the supply path.~Damaged insulation can expose a live conductor and permit an electric-shock path.')
for key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer in [
('5.5-03-M01','Damaged insulation can expose a live conductor.','Contact can allow current through an unintended path including a person.','Recognise exposed wire, cracked casing and frayed cable as reasons to remove equipment from use and obtain appropriate repair. A normal operating appliance can still be unsafe if its insulation is damaged.','State a danger of damaged electrical insulation.','An exposed live conductor can cause electric shock.'),
('5.5-03-M02','Excessive current can overheat cables, connections or sockets.','Several loads on one supply path add their current demands.','A socket count alone is not the physical cause: total load and the ratings of the common path matter. A loose connection can also heat locally. A protective rating should not be increased to hide repeated tripping.','State a danger of overloading a supply socket.','Excessive total current can overheat the common wiring and cause fire.'),
('5.5-03-M03','Damp conditions can increase electrical-shock risk.','Moisture can lower resistance along unintended paths.','The same voltage can drive a larger current through a lower-resistance path. Keep the physics explanation tied to altered conduction rather than saying water simply attracts electricity.','State why damp conditions can increase electric-shock risk.','They can lower the resistance of unintended current paths.')]:add(key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer)
add('5.5-04','Earthing, insulation and overcurrent interruption serve complementary protective roles.','Correct placement and rating are necessary for a protective device to act as intended.','An earth connection provides a low-resistance fault path from suitable exposed metal. A fuse or overcurrent breaker can interrupt excessive current; a residual-current protective device responds to imbalance and is a different device. Double insulation instead prevents accessible conductive parts becoming live without relying on a protective earth in that design. The syllabus discussion is a circuit model, not a complete installation standard.','Explain why earthing and an overcurrent device can work together in a metal-cased appliance model.','Link a live-to-case fault to a low-resistance earth path.~The resulting fault current can operate the correctly selected overcurrent protection and disconnect the supply.')
add('5.5-04-M01','Protective earthing connects suitable exposed metal to a low-resistance protective path.','A live-to-case fault can then produce a fault current that operates suitable protection.','The earth conductor is not intended to carry the normal load current. It helps keep accessible metal near earth potential and supports automatic disconnection in the model. It does not replace intact insulation or prove a damaged appliance is safe to touch.','Describe the protective action of earthing in a metal-case fault model.','The earth path carries fault current from the case so suitable protection can disconnect the supply.')
add('5.5-04-M02','An overcurrent circuit breaker opens a circuit when its designed current-time limit is exceeded.','Its setting must protect the relevant cable and equipment while allowing intended operation.','Unlike a melted fuse link, many breakers can be reset after the fault is dealt with. A higher setting is not automatically better because it may permit dangerous heating. Do not treat every breaker as a residual-current detector; the response depends on the device type.','Describe the role of a correctly selected overcurrent circuit breaker.','It interrupts excessive current before the protected circuit suffers unacceptable heating under its designed response.')
add('5.5-04-M03','A single-pole switch is placed in the live conductor in the syllabus safety model.','Opening it then separates the appliance’s internal load from the live supply side.','A switch only in neutral could stop normal current yet leave internal parts connected to live. Therefore no current and safe isolation are different conditions. Real work requires proper isolation and verification by a competent person, not merely seeing an appliance stop.','Describe why the model places a single-pole switch in live rather than neutral.','Opening the live-side switch disconnects the load from live; a neutral-only switch could leave it connected to live.')
add('5.5-04-M04','Double insulation provides two effective insulating barriers or an equivalent protective design.','It does not rely on an exposed-metal protective-earth path in the intended appliance design.','The commonly used identification is a square within a square. A plastic outer case alone does not prove a device meets the double-insulation design. Compare its protective method with an earthed metal case rather than claiming one visible feature removes every risk.','Describe the protective principle of double insulation.','Two insulating barriers or equivalent protection prevent accessible parts becoming live without relying on a protective-earth connection.')
add('5.5-04-M05','A fuse melts to interrupt an excessive-current path according to its rated behaviour.','Choose a specified suitable rating above normal operating current while protecting the cable and equipment.','A school selection question may give available fuse ratings and normal current, then ask for the smallest suitable rating. Real selection must also account for start-up current and device characteristics. A fuse limits overcurrent consequences; it does not make direct live contact safe or protect against every shock current.','Describe how a fuse protects its circuit.','Excessive current heats and melts its link, opening the path according to its designed rating and response.')
add('5.5-05','In the learning model, live supplies energy, neutral forms the normal return and protective earth serves fault protection.','Wire identity must be checked against labelled terminals and the specified colour convention.','For the convention used here, live is brown, neutral blue and earth green/yellow. Older or nonstandard wiring may differ, so colour alone cannot establish real-world safety. Plug diagrams are studied with an unplugged training model; work on actual mains equipment requires competent supervision and verified isolation. A cord grip holds the outer sheath rather than bare inner conductors.','Explain how a labelled unplugged plug model distinguishes neutral from earth.','Link neutral to the normal return path, shown blue in the stated convention.~Earth is the green/yellow protective connection for faults and is not the normal load return.')
add('5.5-05-M01','A safe training-plug assembly uses correct labelled terminals, secure insulation and an effective cord grip.','The outer cable sheath is held by the grip, with no exposed stray strands.','With the model disconnected from any supply, match each conductor to its labelled terminal using the stated convention, secure the connections, check no bare wire lies outside terminals, clamp the outer sheath and replace the cover. Any fuse must be the specified suitable type and rating. This is an inspection sequence for a supervised training model.','Describe the final cable-grip and conductor checks on an unplugged training plug model.','Check correct labelled terminals, secure enclosed conductors without stray strands, and a cord grip holding the outer sheath.')
add('5.5-05-M02','The colour convention used here is brown live, blue neutral and green/yellow protective earth.','The three functions remain distinct even when a load is switched off.','Live is the conductor disconnected by the model’s single-pole switch; neutral carries the normal return current; earth is protective. These descriptions refer to the stated model and do not certify a real cable by appearance. Label the function as well as the colour in a revision diagram.','Describe the three wire colours and their functions in the stated plug model.','Brown is live supply, blue is normal neutral return and green/yellow is protective earth.')
add('5.5-06','Reducing unnecessary operating time and using efficient devices can reduce electrical energy use for the same intended service.','Energy savings follow E = Pt and the useful-output efficiency.','Switch off an unused light, use suitable efficient lighting, and limit unnecessary heating or cooling. Compare devices providing the same service rather than assuming a lower power always meets the same need. A lower-power heater taking much longer may not save the energy expected from power alone.','Explain how reducing an appliance’s operating time saves energy at constant power.','Energy is power multiplied by time.~A shorter operating interval therefore reduces total energy transfer.')
C=A.choice
C('5.4-01','What is power?',('Energy transfer per time','It is measured in watts.'),('Energy per charge','That is voltage.'),('Charge per time','That is current.'),('Total energy regardless of time','Duration matters for power.'))
C('5.4-03','What does kWh measure?',('Energy','It is power multiplied by time.'),('Power per hour','That is not its meaning.'),('Current','Current uses amperes.'),('Resistance','Resistance uses ohms.'))
C('5.5-02-M02','Why use parallel lamps for independent lighting?',('Each branch can be switched separately','An open branch need not break the others.'),('Each must receive half the voltage','The branches span the same two nodes and therefore have equal voltage.'),('Current is consumed before the second lamp','Charge is conserved.'),('One broken branch must stop every other','That is a series-path problem.'))
C('5.5-04-M03','Where is the single-pole switch placed in the model?',('Live conductor','It disconnects the load from live.'),('Only the earth conductor','That would interrupt protection.'),('Only neutral for complete isolation','The load could remain connected to live.'),('Outside every electrical path','It could not interrupt current.'))
C('5.5-04-M04','Which marking commonly identifies double insulation?',('A square within a square','It denotes the protective design.'),('A letter A in a circle','That is an ammeter symbol.'),('Unequal cell plates','That is a source symbol.'),('A lone earth wire necessarily','Double insulation does not rely on that path.'))
C('5.5-05-M02','Which wire is protective earth in the stated colour convention?',('Green/yellow','This identifies the protective conductor.'),('Blue','This denotes neutral.'),('Brown','This denotes live.'),('A bare exposed strand as normal insulation','Bare exposed strands are not a colour convention.'))
C('5.5-03-M03','Why can damp conditions worsen shock risk?',('They can lower path resistance','At the same voltage this can allow more current.'),('They make current lose its charge','Charge is conserved.'),('They remove voltage by definition','Moisture does not guarantee isolation.'),('They make fuses unnecessary','Protection remains necessary.'))
C('5.4-02-M01','What power is 10 V at 0.3 A?',('3 W','Use VI.'),('33.3 W','This divides V by I.'),('0.03 W','This divides I by V.'),('10.3 W','This adds unlike quantities.'))
C('5.4-02-M03','What energy is 0.8 kW for 2 h?',('1.6 kWh','Multiply power by time.'),('0.4 kWh','This divides by time.'),('2.8 kWh','This adds unlike quantities.'),('1600 kWh','This treats kW as W without conversion.'))
C('5.4-04','At an invented N$3 per kWh, what does 2 kWh cost in energy charges?',('N$6','Multiply energy by the given tariff.'),('N$1.50','This divides tariff by energy.'),('N$5','This adds values with unlike units.'),('N$2','This ignores tariff.'))
C('5.4-02-M02','A 6 V, 1 A heater runs for 10 s. What energy is transferred?',('60 J','Use VIt.'),('6 J','This omits duration.'),('0.6 J','This divides by duration.'),('600 J','This adds an extra factor of ten.'))
C('5.5-04-M05','A model load normally takes 2.0 A. Given suitable choices 1 A, 3 A, 10 A and 13 A, which is the smallest rating above its normal current?',('3 A','This is the stated school selection criterion.'),('1 A','It is below normal current.'),('10 A','A smaller suitable option is given.'),('13 A','It is not the smallest suitable given option.'))
P=A.part
P('5.4-01','State the unit of power.','Watt.',1,'One mark for watt or W.')
P('5.4-03','State whether kWh measures energy or power.','Energy.',1,'One mark for energy.')
P('5.5-04-M03','Explain why a neutral-only switch is not equivalent to the live-side switch in the model.','It can stop current but leave the appliance connected to live. The live-side switch separates the load from that live connection.',2,'One mark for remaining live connection; one for live-side disconnection.')
P('5.4-02-M03','Calculate energy used by a 1.5 kW heater for 2 h.','E = Pt = 3 kWh.',2,'One mark for Pt in matching units; one for 3 kWh.')
P('5.4-04','Calculate energy-only cost for 3 kWh at an invented N$2.20 per kWh.','Cost = 3 × 2.20 = N$6.60.',2,'One mark for energy times tariff; one for N$6.60.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot measured electrical energy vertically against elapsed time horizontally for a fixed-power low-voltage heater.',unit='J/s or W',meaning='The electrical-energy versus time gradient is electrical input power. It is not automatically useful heating power because some energy can leave the intended sample.',method=['Connect a rated low-voltage heater to a current-limited supply with a suitable electrical energy meter.','Place the heater in its intended safe load and record the meter’s initial reading.','Start timing when the heater is energised.','Record cumulative energy at several elapsed times while monitoring voltage and current stability.','Repeat the run and compare energy-time slopes.'],control='Keep supply and heater conditions stable, avoid exposed hot elements, keep electrical connections dry and use no mains construction.',table='Record elapsed time / s and electrical energy above the initial reading / J, with voltage and current checks.',conclusion='A straight energy-time line indicates approximately constant electrical power; compare its gradient with measured VI under the stated DC conditions.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
